      \field{abstract}{Abstract The chapter explores the aesthetic dimensions of human-{AI} collaboration in arts, cultural heritage, and academic research, addressing the complex interplay between artificial and human creativity. As {AI} technologies increasingly integrate into creative domains, fundamental questions arise regarding authorship, originality, emotional depth, and aesthetic judgment. This research analyses how {AI}-supported creative works compare to human-created ones, how {AI}-driven aesthetics challenge traditional human-centred notions of beauty and meaning, and how different research perspectives conceptualise {AI}-driven aesthetics within humanistic traditions. The methodology employed in this study is a systematic literature review and analytical framework, synthesising insights from interdisciplinary sources across aesthetic theory, cognitive science, {AI} research, and cultural studies. By structuring the discussion around seven research areas, the study identifies emerging paradoxes, aesthetic transformations, and evolving paradigms that redefine the relationship between {AI} and human creativity. The results highlight three significant findings: (1) {AI} introduces new aesthetic paradoxes, such as authenticity without intent and infinite synthesis without originality; (2) {AI} fosters novel aesthetic categories, including computational excess and predictive aesthetics (including a kitsch trap); and (3) {AI} acts as a meta-creator, shifting creative agency towards algorithmic curation and hybrid human-{AI} processes. The chapter may benefit scholars, artists, cultural theorists, and {AI} researchers by offering new conceptual frameworks for understanding {AI}’s impact on aesthetic judgment in art, cultural heritage, and knowledge production. It encourages further interdisciplinary dialogue on the evolving role of {AI} in shaping human creativity and intellectual inquiry.}
